This study reports an economical and portable point-of-care (POC) monitoring device based on artificial multienzyme cascade systems for multiple detection purposes. The device was made up of a disposable three dimensional microfluidic paper-based analytical device (3D $\upmu\mathrm{PAD})$ with multiple detection zones and a smart­ phone readout. On-paper synthesis of a multifunctional mimetic composite, based on the $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ nanoparticles embedded in the amino-functionalized Fe metal-organic frameworks $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe)), for cascade reactions was the main achievement of this work. The 3D $\upmu\mathrm{PAD}$ was applied for simultaneous quantification of glucose, fructose, sucrose and maltose, and the detection process consisted of the enzymatic reaction of each sugar by anchored enzymes on the metal-organic frameworks (MOF) and successive oxidation of $^{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}$ -tet­ ramethylbenzidine (TMB). Utilizing the new artificial mimicking system improved the color development uni­ formity and resulted in a reliable detection tool, with excellent detection limits in the range of $20{-}280\upmu\mathrm{M}$ . It was directly applied to analyze the sugars levels of human total blood, urine, semen, honey and juice samples with the relative errors of less than $7.7\%$ compared with the HPLC method. The cost-effective and easy-to-use $\upmu\mathrm{PAD}$ has a great potential to be used in either medical diagnostics or the food industry. Also, it can be considered as a competitive POC method for patients in disadvantaged communities or emergencies.
## 1. Introduction
Microfluidic paper-based analytical devices $(\upmu\mathrm{PAD})$ have been recently one of the most favorable monitoring systems in the food control industry, medicinal diagnostics, and environmental discoveries. Since its introduction in 2007 (Martinez et al., 2007), $\upmu\mathrm{PAD}$ has expe­ rienced tremendous progress. This notable interest is because of its distinct advantages, including low-cost, simplicity, biodegradability, equipment-free, portability, fast response, low sample consumption, proper for use in remote locations, and user-friendly procedure (Akyazi et al., 2018; Xia et al., 2016). Inspired by these fascinating features, $\upmu\mathrm{PADs}$ can be considered as a perfect platform for point-of-care (POC) testing, especially in deprived communities or in emergency situations (Feng et al., 2021; Gong and Sinton, 2017; Xia et al., 2016). The equipment-free and low-cost colorimetric method with rapid imple­ mentation is the most desired detection system used for $\upmu\mathrm{PADs}$ . It is based on a color change caused by a certain chemical reaction, and semi-quantitative analysis can be simply achieved by visually comparing the color changes with some calibration data. More sensitive quantifi­ cations are possible with the assistance of a digital camera, scanner, or cell phone to record the color changes, which are then analyzed by software and connected to the analyte concentration (Fu and Wang, 2018). Although significant applications of $\upmu\mathrm{PADs}$ with colorimetric detection, there are still some deficiencies, such as low sensitivity, coffee ring effect and non-uniform color distribution (Morbioli et al., 2017).
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have been recently acknowledged as a prominent class of crystalline porous materials and promising microenvironment for immobilizing and supporting biomolecules with minimum loss in their conformation against denaturation conditions (Furukawa et al., 2013; Kumar et al., 2015). Besides, they can be also useful to enhance the performance of enzymatic reactions (Xia et al., 2020). Recently, our group reported the immobilization of glucose ox­ idase (GOX) in a two dimensional MOF (2D MOF), which caused great improvements in both stability and performance of the enzyme (Al Lawati and Hassanzadeh, 2020). Similarly, the improved stability of the encapsulated GOX in Fe-MIL-88B– ${\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}$ (MIL stands for Materials of
Institut Lavoisier) and MOF-545(Fe) has been reported (Xu et al., 2019; Zhong et al., 2020). Besides, the potential ability of MOFs to catalyze biomimetic reactions has inspired researchers to examine their appli­ cability as an alternative for high-cost and low-stable enzymes (Li et al., 2018). The reports have also shown that the catalytic activity of MOFs is usually improved by integration of the certain nanomaterials into their pores (Li et al., 2018; Zhang et al., 2019). The formed composites can surmount the lack of active sites on the MOF and cause synergistic features, which are not accessible by individual compartments (Li et al., 2018). For example, cobalt oxide nanoparticles $(\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}N P s})$ embedded in CuMOF (Amirzehni et al., 2021), magnetic $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-101(Fe) composite (Xing et al., 2020), and cobalt oxide nanoparticles (Co NPs) encapsulated in a MOF (Dong et al., 2018), with improved peroxidase-like activity have been reported in the literature.
Monitoring of sugars in biological samples is of great importance. Glucose monitoring is crucial for controlling blood sugar, diagnosing diabetes, islet cell carcinoma and other diseases (Li et al., 2015). Fruc­ tose analysis can provide information about the secretory function of seminal vesicles and locating obstructions in the male reproductive tract (von Eckardstein et al., 2000). Besides, the reports have indicated the association between blood fructose level and pancreatic cancer (Hui et al., 2009). Sucrose is usually measured in the serum or urine as a marker of gastric damage (Tooley et al., 2009). The maltose, as the major product of amylase reaction, is commonly used as a marker to assess the activity of $\upalpha$ -amylase, which is important for the diagnosis of acute pancreatitis, mumps, renal disease and abdominal disorders (Wang et al., 2015). Besides, information on the concentration of monoand disaccharides in drinks and food is important for the assessment of their quality (Aksorn and Teepoo, 2020).
In this work, an integrated smartphone-based disposable 3D paperbased colorimetric device has been reported for simultaneous mea­ surement of various sugars in food and biological samples. The multiplex detection was achieved by distributing samples from the sample zone to four detection zones (G, F, S, and M for glucose, fructose, sucrose and maltose, respectively) using a distributing layer between the sample injection and detection layers (Fig. 1). The modification of detection zones was performed by direct synthesis of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe) using a room temperature method in the presence of $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ nanoparticles (NPs). Then, each detection zone was loaded by $^{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}$ -tetramethylbenzidine (TMB), as well as specific enzymes for each sugar. The formed $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B/TMB/enzyme composite acted as an artificial mimicking cascade system to produce a colored product on the paper. A cellphone was applied to take the photographs of the resultant colors, which were then assessed by ImageJ. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first report on the synthesis of an artificial mimicking system on paper for multiplex detection purposes. The simple fabrication process, low cost, easy production in large scale, high stability, and low sample consumption provide a competitive method for POC medicinal diagnosis and food control applications, especially for the underserved communities.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Materials and instruments
The chemicals utilized in the presented work were all of the analytical grades and no further purification was done. Glucose oxidase (GOX, 100 units/mg), D-fructose dehydrogenase (FDH, 400–1200 units/ mg), invertase (IVT, 300 Unit/mg), Maltase (MT, 50 Unit/mg), and horseradish peroxidase (HRP, 250 units/mg) were obtained from Sigma (USA). The $5\mathrm{mM}$ stock solution of $^{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}$ -tetramethylbenzidine (TMB) was freshly prepared in methanol. The enzyme solutions $(5~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1})$ were individually prepared in buffer solution $\mathrm{(KH_{2}P O_{4}/K_{2}H P O_{4}}$ , $0.03\mathrm{M}$ , pH 7); they were stable for at least 2 weeks at $2^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ Dilution of enzyme solutions was done with the same buffer when required.
An HP LaserJet p1102 was used to print the designs on the filter papers (Whatman, United Kingdom). Faber-Castell wax crayons, pur­ chased from a local supermarket in Muscat, were used to form hydro­ phobic barriers and create separate chambers. Then, the wax-patterned papers were heated on a heater $(180^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ for a short time (about 2 s) to spread the molten wax in the paper. The wax-printed ${3\mathrm{D}\upmu\mathrm{P}\mathrm{AD}}$ consisted of three layers: an injection layer with a circle hydrophilic zone $\mathrm{{(7mm}}$ in diameter), a distributing layer to distribute the injected sample toward the different detection zones on the third layer, as the detection layer. Assembling the ${3\mathrm{D}\upmu\mathrm{PAD}}$ is illustrated in Fig. 1. Two simple transparent sheets, printed by a 3D printer (MiiCraft), and two clips were used to fix the assembled $\upmu\mathrm{PAD}$ . The photos from $\upmu\mathrm{PAD}$ were taken by a Huawei $\mathtt{P20P r o}$ phone (China) and processed by ImageJ software. The average color intensity of each detection zone, stated as relative light units (RLU), was related to the concentration of considered sugar. The photos were taken in a fixed condition to achieve high resolution, clear images, and reproducible results.
More information about the material and instruments is provided in Supporting Information.
## 2.2. Synthesis of metal-organic frameworks
## 2.5. Real samples
## 2.2.1. Preparation of MOF in solution
To prepare $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe), ethanol was used as the solvent. Briefly, 1 mmol of $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ and 1 mmol of 2-amino-1,4-benzene dicarboxylic acid $2\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ -BDC) were mixed in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of ethanol and stirred for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ at room temperature. The light brown product was collected by centrifugation (5 min, $6000~\mathrm{rpm})$ , and the unreacted re­ agents were eliminated by dimethylformamide (DMF). Then, the washing process by ethanol was done three times and the product was finally dried under vacuum at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{6\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The same procedure was applied to form $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe), except an appropriate amount of $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ NPs was added into the initial mixture of cations-ligands, before the crystal grew. To obtain the composite with 3, 6, and $10\%\mathsf{C e O}_{2}\mathsf{N P s}.$ , the amount of 8, 15, and $30\mathrm{mg}$ of the NPs were used, respectively. Also, MIL-88B(Fe) and $\mathrm{CeO_{2}@M I L^{-}}88\mathrm{B(Fe)}$ without amino groups were pro­ duced by using 1,4-benzene dicarboxylic acid (BDC), as the coordination ligand.
## 2.2.2. Preparation of MOF on paper
To grow the MOF crystals on the paper, $10\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of the mixed $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ and $2{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -BDC solution (in the presence or absence of $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ NPs) was immediately transferred to each detection zone and left for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ (in a covered box to prevent the evaporation of ethanol solvent). The pro­ duced crystals were washed with $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ DMF (left for $10\mathrm{min}\mathrm{.}$ ) and then with $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ ethanol (for $5~\mathrm{min}$ , three times), respectively. The drying process was also accomplished by transferring the paper in an oven $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $3\mathrm{h}$ , under vacuum).
## 2.3. Preparation of paper-based devices
After coating the detection zones with $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe), a 4 μL of TMB solution $\mathrm{~\textit~{~S~mM~}~}$ in methanol) was dropped on each zone, and left for $5~\mathrm{{min}}$ to dry. Then, $4~{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ of enzyme solution prepared by dis­ solving special enzymes (depending on the considered analyte) in phosphate buffer (0.05 M, pH 7) was added on the detection zone. GOX $(100\mathrm{~U~mL}^{-1})$ ), FDH $(50\mathrm{~U~mL}^{-1})$ , mixed GOX/IVT $(80\mathrm{~U~mL}^{-1})$ , and mixed GOX/MT $(100~\mathrm{U~mL^{-1}})$ were used for the detection of glucose, fructose, sucrose and maltose, respectively. After drying for $10\mathrm{min}$ , the prepared $\upmu\mathrm{PAD}$ was assembled and preserved at $4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . It was stable for at least 70 days.
HRP-modified $\upmu\mathrm{PAD}$ was also prepared without coating by MOF; After adding TMB and the considered sugar enzyme, the HRP enzyme (4 $\mathrm{\upmuL},0.02\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}.$ ) was added on the detection zones (stable for 1 week).
## 2.4. Application of paper-based device for the detection of sugars
A small volume $(60~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ of the mixed standard solutions containing different sugars, (or prepared real sample) was dropped on the injection zone. The solution can flow through the channels, designed on the second layer, and reach detection zones on the third layer. The gener­ ated colors were recorded using the smartphone, $3~\mathrm{min}$ after sample injection. The photos were then evaluated by ImageJ software and the mean color intensity (RLU) in each detection zone was related to the concentration of the corresponding sugar in the sample using a cali­ bration graph.
For total blood samples, a small Whatman $\textsuperscript{\textregistered}$ glass microfiber filter, Grade GF/A, (diameter of $8\mathrm{mm}^{\cdot}$ ) was used on the injection zone. It could hinder the blood cell to reach the detection zones, and eliminate the interfering effect of the red color of blood cells on the monitoring of produced blue color. $30~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ of blood sample (or standard solution of sugars) was added on the GF filter, and followed by adding $30~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ phosphate buffer solution $\left(\mathrm{pH}7\right)$ , which can deliver sugars toward the detection zones.
Blood samples were taken from healthy volunteers and directly analyzed by prepared $\upmu\mathrm{PAD}$ . Semen samples, obtained from healthy volunteers, were used after their liquefaction by preserving in poly­ propylene sterile containers $(25\mathrm{\min})$ . Then, they were centrifuged 0 $5000~\mathrm{rpm}$ , $10~\mathrm{{min}}.$ and the separated seminal plasma was applied for the assessment of fructose and glucose using the developed $\upmu\mathrm{PAD}$ . Urine samples were also analyzed after their filtration through a syringe filter $(0.45~{\upmu\mathrm{m}})$ .
Omani local honey samples were directly obtained from beekeepers. The samples were directly analyzed after their proper dilution; $_{0.1\mathrm{~g~}}$ of each sample was dissolved in $25~\mathrm{mL}$ . Juice samples (from a local su­ permarket in Muscat) were also filtered through the $0.45~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ syringe filters, and $_{1\mathrm{~g~}}$ of each sample was dissolved in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ deionized water.
## 3. Results and discussion
Considering separate layers for the sample injection and detection aims is useful for the easy and reproducible preparation of ${\upmu}\mathrm{{PADs}}.$ because the immobilization of the reagents on the detection zone could be done without their diffusion into other parts. Herein, a distributing layer (red color) was applied between the injection layer (yellow color) and detection layer (orange color) (Fig. 1), consisting of a central circle and four micro-channels $\left(1\ \mathrm{mm}\right)$ in different directions to receive and deliver the sample toward the delivery circles, which are placed just above the detection zones. The excess volume of sample is adsorbed by some waste circles which are also considered on the distributing layer. This prevents the collecting of sample solution in the detection zones, removing the coffee ring effect and improving the color uniformity. Besides, the direct synthesis of $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe) on the paper further improved the uniformity of the color development on the detection zones. The composite also showed an improved and syner­ gistic mimetic activity, which was beneficial to increase the sensitivity. Moreover, it notably enhanced the stability of the immobilized enzymes, increasing the storage lifetime of $\upmu\mathrm{PAD}$ . The newly designed 3D $\upmu\mathrm{PAD}$ resulted in a fully sample-to-answer and low-cost monitoring device for practical on-site and POC applications.
## 3.1. Characterization of the prepared material
The scanning electron microscope (SEM) image showed the 2D morphology of the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe) prepared on the paper (Fig. 2A). The presence of $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ NPs did not affect the morphology of the final product (Fig. 2B), however, the formed composite showed a rough surface, due to the embedded $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ NPs. Fig. 2C also shows the SEM image of $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe) loaded by TMB and enzyme (GOX), with almost similar morphology. Transmission electron spectroscope (TEM) analysis (Fig. 2D and F) verified the obtained results; 2D MOF crystals with a thickness of lower than $10\mathrm{nm}$ were observed. Moreover, the HR-TEM image of $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe) indicated lattice dspacing of 0.96 and $0.85~\mathrm{nm}$ , corresponding to the (101) and (002) planes of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe), respectively (Fig. 2F and Fig. S1). Also, the d-spacing values of 0.31 and $0.19~\mathrm{nm}$ were related to the (111) and (220) planes of fluorite-type $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ (Lian et al., 2019).
Fig. 2G displays the X-ray diffraction (XRD) spectra of the synthe­ sized materials. The main diffraction peaks for $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe) were observed at $9.1^{\circ}$ (002), $10.2^{\circ}$ (101), $12.9^{\circ}$ (102), $16.6^{\circ}$ (103) and $20.4^{\circ}$ (201) (Shi et al., 2015). The synthesis of MOF in the presence of $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ NPs, caused some additional diffraction peaks at the 2θ values of $28.5^{\circ}$ (111), $32.9^{\circ}$ (200), $47.4^{\circ}$ (220), and $56.2^{\circ}$ (311), which are ascribed to the presence of $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ NPs. After modification of the composite with enzyme (GOX), no difference appeared in the XRD pattern.
The results of Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) anal­ ysis were also shown in Fig. 2H. The main absorption bands of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL88B(Fe) were located at about $570~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (Fe–O), $3420~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (N–H),
$1626~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ $(0-\mathsf{C}\mathrm{=}0)$ , and $1485~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (C–O). After the integration of $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}\mathsf{N P s}$ , the formed nanocomposite displayed an intense peak for the Ce–O bond at about $480\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . No remarkable changes were observed in the FTIR spectrum of $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe) by loading TMB, except some small peaks at $3098~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1106~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ , related to the aromatic C–H and $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-N}$ bonds in TMB molecules, respectively. By introducing enzyme (GOX) on the composite, special absorption peaks of amide (I) and amide (II) groups appeared at $1711~\mathrm{{\cm}}^{-1}$ and $1234~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ , respectively.
Investigation of the $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ adsorption/desorption isotherms of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL88B and $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe) revealed a multilayer adsorption process (type IV isotherms), with Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface areas of 653.28 and $296.05\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. The combination with $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ NPs notably decreased the surface area of the composite. Besides, compared with $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}@\mathrm{MIL}\cdot88\mathrm{B}(\mathrm{Fe})$ , the presence of amino-functional groups had an improving effect on the surface area of $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ - MIL-88B(Fe), however, it diminished the average pore size (Table S1).
X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) data verified the existence of C, N, O, and Fe elements in the structure of both $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B (Fe) and $\mathrm{NH_{2}}\mathrm{-MIL}\mathrm{-}88\mathrm{B}$ (Fig. S2). Furthermore, Ce 3d peaks also appeared for $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe). The C 1S spectra could be deconvoluted to three individual peaks at 284.7, 286.3 and $288.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ , linked to the C–C, C–O–C, and $0{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{C}{\mathrm{=}}0$ bonds, respectively (Xu et al., 2021), and no remarkable changes were found in the presence of $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$
NPs (Fig. S2A). The main portion $(>85\%)$ ) of the O 1s spectrum of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe) was related to C–O–Fe $(532.2\mathrm{eV})$ , as well as two small peaks at $530.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ (Fe–O–Fe) and 533.1 eV (Fe–O–H) (Fig. S2B). How­ ever, the intensity of the peak at $530.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ was sensibly increased for $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe), due to the presence of Ce–O–Ce bonds in $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ NPs. There was no remarkable difference between the N 1S XPS spectra of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe) and $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe); the peaks at 399.2 $\left(\mathrm{C}\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\right)$ and $400.6\ \mathrm{eV}$ $\left(\mathrm{C-N}\mathrm{H}_{3}^{+}\right).$ ) demonstrated the presence of amine groups. (Fig. S2C). Two main peaks at 711.7 (Fe $\scriptstyle2{\mathrm{p}}_{3/2})$ and 725.1 eV (Fe $\boldsymbol{2}\mathrm{p}_{1/2})$ in the XPS spectra of both $\mathrm{\DeltaNH_{2}}$ -MIL-88B(Fe) and $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}@\mathrm{NH}_{2^{-}}$ ­ MIL-88B(Fe) were related to Fe (III) (Xu et al., 2021). Finally, $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe) also indicated the special XPS peaks of $\mathsf{C e}(\mathrm{IV})$ , related to the $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ (at 882.4, 889.7, and 899.1 eV) and $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ (901.5, 909.8, and $918.7\ \mathrm{eV})$ spin-orbit splitting (Fig. S2E) (Lian et al., 2019). Besides, two peaks at binding energies of 889.8 $(3\mathrm{d}_{5/2})$ and 903.0 $(3\mathrm{d}_{3/2})$ verified the presence of a small percentage of Ce (III) species $(13\%>)$ in the structure of $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe).
## 3.2. Mimetic activity of the synthesized composite
The enzyme-like activity of $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ NPs has been reported in the literature (Huang et al., 2018). This potential activity is may related to the simultaneous presence of $\mathsf{C e}^{3+}$ and $\mathsf{C e}^{4+}$ in $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ NPs and their intrinsic ability to generate active radicals in the reaction media. Herein, the catalytic activity of $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe) was evaluated using the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ -TMB reaction. Fig. 3A shows the absorption spectra of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ -TMB solution after reacting for $10\mathrm{min}$ in the presence of different catalysts. The results obtained on the paper are also indicated as inset. No obvious color change was observed in the absence of any catalysts. Besides, $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe) could not oxidize TMB in the absence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2},$ , indicating that it cannot act as an oxidase-like nanoenzyme. However, adding $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe) into the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ -TMB system quickly caused the blue color, which verifies its excellent peroxidase-like activity. On the other hand, $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe) caused the most intensive signals for both solution and paper-based experiments, compared with its components, $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ NPs and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe). This demonstrates the superior synergistic catalytic activity of the integrated composite. The results also showed the improved activity of amino-functionalized composite compared to $\mathrm{CeO_{2}}@\mathrm{MIL}\cdot88\mathrm{B}(\mathrm{Fe})$ without amine groups, which can be explained based on its relatively high surface area (Table S1).
Compared with HRP, the color change for $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe)- coated $\upmu\mathrm{PAD}$ occurs in a shorter time, and the color intensity was higher. Moreover, the uniformity of the color development on the paper was remarkably improved by using $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe), as the sub­ strate (Fig. 3B).
The catalyzing activity of the ethanol phase prepared composite $\mathrm{(CeO_{2}@N H_{2}}$ -MIL-88B(Fe)-E) was also compared with the activity of the composite prepared in DMF at high temperature $\mathrm{(CeO_{2}@N H_{2}}$ -MIL-88B (Fe)-D) (Fig. S3). The solution-based experiments with $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL88B(Fe)-D showed relatively higher signals, however, it is not possible to synthesize it directly on the paper. Coating the paper with the synthe­ sized $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe)-D also declined the uniformity of the color development.
As the control experiments, some experiments on the paper were also done using $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ and $\mathsf{C e}^{4+}$ , as catalysts (Fig. S3). The relatively weak color changes for these catalysts demonstrated the usefulness of the prepared composite to achieve a high efficient $\upmu\mathrm{PAD}$ .
Finally, it was observed that the injection of solutions with low $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ concentrations on the $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe)-coated $\upmu\mathrm{PAD}$ causes clear color changes, which are proportional to the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ concentration (Fig. 3D).
## 3.3. Steady-state kinetic studies
Kinetic studies based on the Michaelis-Menten equation were accomplished using the TMB- $\mathrm{{H}}_{2}\mathrm{{O}}_{2}$ solution-based reaction. Two series of solutions in the constant conditions were prepared for each catalyst: the first series contained different $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ concentrations and the second series contained different TMB concentrations. The change in the absorbance of each solution $\left(652\mathrm{nm}\right)$ was followed, and the initial rates of reactions were used to plot the Michaelis-Menten curve. The results for $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe) are indicated in Fig. S4. The calculated $\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{m}}$ (Michaelis-Menten constant) and $\mathtt{V}_{\mathtt{m a x}}$ (maximum reaction rate) values for each catalyst are shown in Table S2, approving the relatively high catalytic efficiency of $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe); the low $\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{m}}$ values show the high tendency of the reagents $\mathrm{{(H_{2}O_{2}}}$ and TMB) toward the surface of the catalyst. Thus, $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}@\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe) can be considered as a po­ tential alternative for HRP, due to its great advantages, such as higher efficiency, low cost, great stability, and easy preservation.
## 3.4. Possible mechanism
According to the reports and based on the obtained results, a po­ tential mechanism for the mimetic activity of $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe) is illustrated as follows:
$$
\begin{array}{r l}&{\mathbf{F}\mathbf{e}^{3^{+}}+\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}\rightarrow\mathbf{F}\mathbf{e}^{2^{+}}+\mathbf{H}\mathbf{O}_{2}^{*}+\mathbf{H}^{+}}\\ &{\mathbf{H}\mathbf{O}_{2}^{*}\rightarrow\mathbf{O}_{2}^{-*}+\mathbf{H}^{+}}\\ &{\mathbf{O}_{2}^{-*}+\mathbf{F}\mathbf{e}^{3^{+}}\rightarrow\mathbf{O}_{2}+\mathbf{F}\mathbf{e}^{2^{+}}}\\ &{\mathbf{H}\mathbf{O}_{2}^{*}+\mathbf{F}\mathbf{e}^{3^{+}}\rightarrow\mathbf{O}_{2}+\mathbf{H}++\mathbf{F}\mathbf{e}^{2^{+}}}\\ &{\mathbf{F}\mathbf{e}^{2^{+}}+\mathbf{H}\mathbf{O}_{2}\rightarrow\mathbf{F}\mathbf{e}^{3^{+}}+\mathbf{sOH}+\mathbf{OH}^{-}}\\ &{\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}+\mathbf{sOH}\rightarrow\mathbf{H}\mathbf{O}_{2}^{*}+\mathbf{H}\mathbf{O}}\\ &{\mathbf{C}\mathbf{e}^{3^{+}}+\mathbf{H}\mathbf{O}_{2}\rightarrow\mathbf{C}\mathbf{e}^{4^{+}}+\mathbf{sOH}+\mathbf{OH}^{-}}\end{array}
$$
Active radicals $+\mathrm{TMB}\rightarrow\mathrm{F}$ Blue product
Generally, the catalytic action of the Fe-based catalysts is described based on the generation of the reactive oxidative species (ROSs) via a Fenton-like reaction (Chen et al., 2015). $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ can act as a Lewis base to directly attach to the metal nodes, which can act as Lewis acid sites. This coordination was verified by the quenching effect of $\mathrm{PO_{4}}^{3-}$ on the oxidation of TMB. The $\mathrm{PO_{4}}^{3-}$ anions serve as a stronger Lewis base, and are preferred to be adsorbed on the metal nodes rather than $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ (Liu et al., 2021). In this state, $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ can accelerate the decomposition of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to produce $_{\mathrm{HO}_{2}^{\bullet}}$ radicals (eq. (1)). Transferring the electron in the metal-oxide clusters is probably accelerated by amine groups, facili­ tating the production of $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ (Liu et al., 2021). So, the adsorbed $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ interacts with the created $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ centers via a Fenton-like reaction to produce abundant $\bullet_{\mathrm{OH}}$ (eq. (5)). On the other hand, as shown in XPS spectra, $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ NPs contain a notable fraction of $\mathsf{C e}^{3+}$ ions (Fig. S2E), which can be considered as the efficient surface defects on the surface of nanoparticles. They can act as active sites for catalytic action. The interaction of $\mathsf{C e}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ via a Fenton-like reaction can also produce $\cdot_{\mathrm{OH}}$ (eq. $(7)$ ). The embedding of $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ NPs into the MOF structure prevents their agglomeration and further increases their active surface area. Finally, the produced $\mathrm{HO}_{2}^{\bullet}$ and $\bullet_{\mathrm{OH}}$ radicals can oxidize the colorless TMB to form blue ox-TMB (eq. (8)).
Experiments were done in the presence of t-butyl alcohol (t-BA, as a •OH scavenger) and p-benzoquinone (p-BQ, as a $\cdot_{{0_{2}}^{-}}$ scavenger) to confirm the role of $\cdot_{{0_{2}}^{-}}$ and $\cdot_{\mathrm{OH}}$ in this system. Adding $2\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of t-BA (0.2 mM) and p-BQ $(0.1~\mathrm{mM})$ on the TMB– $\mathrm{.H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2^{-}}\mathrm{CeO}_{2}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe) reaction media reduced the absorption signals by $63\%$ and $79\%$ , respectively. This result approves the presence of both $\cdot_{0_{2}^{-}}$ and $\cdot_{\mathrm{OH}}$ in the catalytic reaction. The role of active radicals was further studied by the electron spin resonance (ESR) method using 5, 5-dimethyl-1-pyrro­ line N-oxide (DMPO), as the spin trapper (Fig. S5). According to the obtained results, both DMPO- $\cdot\mathrm{\DeltaOH}$ and $\mathrm{DMPO–}^{\bullet}\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}$ signals were observed for $\mathrm{CeO_{2}}@\mathrm{NH_{2}}\mathrm{-MIL-}88\mathrm{B}(\mathrm{Fe})\mathrm{-}\mathrm{H_{2}O_{2}}$ system, verifying the key role of both $\cdot_{{0_{2}}^{-}}$ and $\bullet_{\mathrm{OH}}$ radicals during the catalytic action of the composite.
Additionally, the ESR signal intensities obtained for $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ - MIL-88B(Fe) were relatively stronger than that of the $\mathrm{CeO_{2}@M I L-88B}$ (Fe), probably because of the presence of the electron-donating amine group, which can facilitate the reducing $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ and subsequently pro­ ducing $\cdot_{{0_{2}}^{-}}$ radicals. This can be the reason for the high catalytic ac­ tivity of $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe) compared to $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}@\mathrm{MIL}\cdot88\mathrm{B}(\mathrm{Fe})$ ) (Liu et al., 2021).
## 3.5. Optimization experiments
Some comparative experiments were implemented to examine the effects of different factors, including pH, TMB concentration, the volume of the pre-synthesis solution added on the paper, the amount of $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$
NPs used in the synthesis of catalyst, and the amount of enzymes, on the final color intensity. Fig. S6A shows that the maximum color intensity is obtained at the wide pH range of 5–8.5. However, it can be seen that the mimetic activity of $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe) is almost constant at the pH range of 3–11 (Fig. S6B). Thus, the decreased signal of $\upmu\mathrm{PAD}$ at more acidic $\mathrm{(pH}<5\AA)$ or alkaline $\mathrm{(pH>8.5)}$ media can be ascribed to the diminished activity of the embedded enzymes. Also, compared to the mimic $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe), the results of HRP-based $\upmu\mathrm{PAD}$ were significantly affected by changing $\mathfrak{p H}$ , demonstrating the stabilizing effect of the applied composite.
The color intensity of the $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe)-based $\upmu\mathrm{PAD}$ was improved by increasing the concentration of TMB up to $4\mathrm{mM}$ (Fig. S6G). The concentrations higher than $10~\mathrm{mM}$ caused a decrease in the detec­ tion sensitivity, probably due to the occupying of active sites on $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe) by excess TMB. Thus, $\mathsf{5m M}$ was selected as the optimal TMB concentration for further experiments.
As mentioned before, the mixed precursor solution of $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ - MIL-88B(Fe) was used to grow the composite directly on the paper. The best results and the more uniform colors were achieved using $10\mathrm{-}20~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of the composite precursor solution. Also, the maximum sensitivity was related to the $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe) composite prepared by $6\%\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ NPs (Figs. S6D, S6E, and S6F). The higher percentages of $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ NPs can occupy the active surface of the MOF and cause a negative effect on its activity.
For enzymes concentrations (Fig. S6G), the highest signals were also obtained using $100~\mathrm{U~mL^{-1}}$ of GOX (for glucose determination), $50~\mathrm{U}$ $\mathrm{\mL}^{-1}$ of FDH (for fructose), $100\ \mathrm{U}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ of GOX and $80\ \mathrm{U}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ of IVT (for sucrose), and $100\ \mathrm{U}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ of GOX and $100\ \mathrm{U}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ of MT (for maltose).
Finally, the best results were related to the paper devices with detection zones of $7\mathrm{mm}$ in diameter (Fig. S6H) that were loaded by the reagents in an order of $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe), TMB, enzymes. The reaction was completed in $3\mathrm{min}$ after the addition of the sugars sample, and the generated blue color could be recorded and analyzed in 3–40 min (Fig. S6I).
## 3.6. Analytical figures of merit
The results for the measurement of the standard sugar solutions with different concentrations using the developed 3D $\upmu\mathrm{PAD}$ , as well as the calibration graphs, are shown in Fig. 4A. The obtained signals were proportional to the concentrations of glucose, fructose, sucrose, and maltose in the ranges of $100~{\upmu\mathrm{M}}{-}10~\mathrm{mM}$ , $50~{\upmu\mathrm{M}}{-}8~\mathrm{mM}$ , $0.2\mathrm{-}5\ \mathrm{mM}$ , and $0.5{-}8~\mathrm{mM}$ , respectively. Limit of detection (LOD) and limit of quantifi­ cation values were also calculated using the formulas $3S_{\mathrm{b}}/\mathrm{m}$ and $10\mathrm{{S_{b}}/\Omega}$ m, respectively, where the $s_{\mathrm{b}}$ represents the standard deviation value for the blank solution signals repeated 5 times, and m is referred to the slope of the corresponding linear calibration graph (Table S3). For blood samples, only $30~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ of total blood was injected on the injection zone, followed by the addition of $30~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ of buffer solution to deliver sugar content toward the detection zones; the obtained figures of merit are summarized in Table S3. The sensitivity of the designed 3D $\upmu\mathrm{PAD}$ is suitable for monitoring the sugars level in the selected real samples. Besides, the reproducibility of the developed device was investigated by repeating the determination of each sugar with a constant concentration 5 times. The obtained relative standard deviation $(\mathrm{RSD\%})$ values (Table S3) demonstrated the acceptable precision of the method.
A comparison between the obtained figures of merit for the devel­ oped 3D $\upmu\mathrm{PAD}$ and the other reported detection systems for sugars is provided in Table S4. Despite the rapid and simple detection procedure, the presented system showed comparable linear ranges and LOD values. This is due to the great catalytic activity of the new $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B (Fe) composite synthesized on the paper, as well as its outstanding stabilizing effect on the embedded enzymes. The method can be considered as a low-cost, rapid, and reliable tool for on-site and simul­ taneously monitoring of different sugars level in various samples.
## 3.7. Specificity
To investigate the interfering effects of some compounds, which may exist in the real samples, determination of each sugar in the presence of interfering compounds was done on the separate paper devices (Fig. S7). No sensible changes were observed in the obtained analytical signals, demonstrating the high selectivity of the designed monitoring tool. The embedded specific enzymes for each sugar are responsible for this selectivity.
## 3.8. Stability
To evaluate the stability and the storage lifetime of the prepared 3D $\upmu\mathrm{PAD}$ , it was precisely covered in parafilm and maintained at $4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Then, it was applied for the analysis of sugars on different days. Figs. S8A and S8B indicate the results for the HRP and $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe) modified ${\upmu}\mathrm{{PADs}}$ , respectively. It is clear that the application of $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe) remarkably improved the lifetime of the pre­ pared ${\upmu}\mathrm{{PADs}}$ . The signals for HRP-modified $\upmu\mathrm{PADs}$ were decreased after 7 days of its storage at $4^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ , however, the $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe) modified ${\upmu}\mathrm{{PADs}}$ were stable for at least 10 weeks. These results demonstrate the stabilizing effect of $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe) on the embedded enzymes; they retained $94\%$ of the initial activity after 10 weeks.
The stability of $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe) synthesized on the paper was also examined by the TMB- $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ system, in the absence of enzymes (Fig. S8C). The results showed a constant signal 100 days after its preparation, verifying the high stability of the composite.
## 3.9. Real samples
## 4. Conclusion
The experimental synthesis of a multifunctional 2D $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL88B(Fe) composite on a paper substrate was accomplished at room temperature. The composite showed synergistic peroxidase-like activity and was able to dissociate $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to form active radicals, which then oxidized the TMB substrate on the paper. The enhanced activity can be explained by the presence of $\mathsf{C e}^{3+}$ species, which can act as active sites for producing reactive radicals, as well as contributing to more efficient electron transfer in the Fe-oxide clusters. The $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-88B(Fe) composite created a remarkable signal in a short time, compared with the conventional peroxidase catalyst, HRP. Furthermore, the direct synthesis method enabled uniform growth of the composite and better color development on the paper. In addition to its catalyzing role, the composite also provided a safe environment for the embedded enzymes, improving their stability and lifetime. Consequently, the application of this multienzyme cascade system with a smartphone-based readout led to the development of a potent, cheap, and rapid $\upmu\mathrm{PAD}$ for the simul­ taneous measurement of glucose, fructose, sucrose, and maltose in various food and biological samples, which was stable and applicable for at least 70 days. The main challenge of this work was the measurement of low concentrations of sucrose and maltose in samples with a high glucose level. Since glucose oxidase enzyme is required for maltose and sucrose measurement, the presence of high glucose concentrations can influence their detection. However, it is expected that a proper enzy­ matic system can alleviate this issue.
